WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:04.660
Every MCU project comes with at least a few
Easter eggs that nod at the comic book history

00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:05.819
that inspired them.

00:00:05.819 --> 00:00:09.540
From Halloween costumes to big band music,
here are the best Vision Easter eggs you're

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:10.680
going to find in the MCU.

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:16.139
Marvel Studios has a long tradition of simultaneously
poking fun at their own characters and paying

00:00:16.139 --> 00:00:20.810
homage by giving us glimpses of those heroes
in goofier yet more comic book-accurate versions

00:00:20.810 --> 00:00:21.850
of their costumes.

00:00:21.850 --> 00:00:26.070
Edward Norton's Bruce Banner passes on the
purple pants Betty Ross buys him in 2008's

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:30.840
The Incredible Hulk, Jessica Jones is less
than thrilled at the idea of her more comics-accurate

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:35.540
attire in Netflix's Jessica Jones, and Luke
Cage made its hero's science experiment garb

00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:37.500
into his '70s comic book outfit.

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:42.980
Heck, in 2011's Captain America: The First
Avenger, Steve Rogers wears a clownish, comics-accurate

00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:44.810
version of his costume before getting an upgrade.

00:00:44.810 --> 00:00:49.270
In episode 6 of WandaVision, we get a similar
treatment for Paul Bettany's Vision.

00:00:49.270 --> 00:00:53.320
Bowing to Wanda's wishes, Vision dresses for
Halloween as a Mexican wrestler, although

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:57.000
it might be more accurate to say that he's
dressed as a comics accurate Vision.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:00.800
In fact, almost the whole family rocks their
comics-accurate looks

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:04.690
While comics fans might recognize the outfits
right away, it's a little less obvious to

00:01:04.690 --> 00:01:06.610
the characters on WandaVision.

00:01:06.610 --> 00:01:10.500
The false Pietro makes a number of guesses
at what Vision is supposed to be:

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:12.320
"Sweet costume, broham in law.

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:16.250
Lemme guess: A traffic light.

00:01:16.250 --> 00:01:17.250
Half-shucked corn.

00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:18.250
A booger!"

00:01:19.250 --> 00:01:23.460
One of the more interesting Vision Easter
eggs we've seen comes in "Don't Touch That

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:25.890
Dial," the second episode of WandaVision.

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:29.000
Wanda is in her living room when she hears
something crash outside.

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:32.820
In her front yard, she finds what appears
to be a remote control helicopter in the bushes.

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:36.130
Later, in the fourth episode, we learn this
is actually a S.W.O.R.D.

00:01:36.130 --> 00:01:38.170
drone transformed by the Hex.

00:01:38.170 --> 00:01:41.730
The helicopter is one of the first things
in color we see in WandaVision's black-and-white

00:01:41.730 --> 00:01:42.730
world.

00:01:42.730 --> 00:01:46.100
It's red and gold with some metallic silver
mixed in — similar to the color scheme of

00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:47.870
Tony Stark's Iron Man armor.

00:01:47.870 --> 00:01:50.159
The number 57 is on its side, and the S.W.O.R.D.

00:01:50.159 --> 00:01:51.590
symbol is painted on the front.

00:01:51.590 --> 00:01:53.470
How is this a Vision Easter egg?

00:01:53.470 --> 00:01:57.920
Well, the most obvious sign is the number
57 on the side of the helicopter, which matches

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:02.700
the number for the comic in which Vision premieres
— 1968's Avengers #57.

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:07.490
It's also probably not a mistake that the
sitcom most heavily referenced in WandaVision's

00:02:07.490 --> 00:02:13.370
second episode is Bewitched, which premiered
in 1964 and was still going strong in 1968,

00:02:13.370 --> 00:02:16.340
when Vision's first appearance showed up on
comic book spinners.

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:20.410
Plus, the Iron Man colors reference Tony Stark
— one of Vision's creators.

00:02:20.410 --> 00:02:24.950
It also arrives with what sounds like an accidental
crash, and much of the rest of the episode

00:02:24.950 --> 00:02:28.060
involves Vision malfunctioning after swallowing
a piece of chewing gum.

00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:32.370
We first meet Vision in 2015's Avengers: Age
of Ultron.

00:02:32.370 --> 00:02:36.950
But in a sense, we kind of met him four years
earlier in Captain America: The First Avenger.

00:02:36.950 --> 00:02:40.630
Before Steve Rogers goes through the experiment
that makes him a super soldier, he visits

00:02:40.630 --> 00:02:44.680
the World's Fair where — among other things
— he sees what appears to be an inert man

00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:47.130
in a red costume kept in a transparent tube.

00:02:47.130 --> 00:02:51.459
Above the man is a sign that reads, "Phineas
Horton Presents the Synthetic Man."

00:02:51.459 --> 00:02:54.599
This is Jim Hammond, the original Human Torch.

00:02:54.599 --> 00:02:58.321
Long before the introduction of the Fantastic
Four, Marvel Comics had an earlier version

00:02:58.321 --> 00:03:01.800
of the Human Torch who was an android created
by Dr. Phineas Horton.

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:06.040
In fact, it's a battle between the original
Human Torch and the Sub-Mariner that creates

00:03:06.040 --> 00:03:08.130
the concept of the Marvel Universe.

00:03:08.130 --> 00:03:12.489
When they meet and fight in 1940's Marvel
Mystery Comics #8, it's the first time two

00:03:12.489 --> 00:03:15.680
of Marvel's heroes are shown to occupy the
same narrative.

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:21.460
In 1975's Avengers #135, the Vision discovers
that Ultron created him using the brainwave

00:03:21.460 --> 00:03:24.310
patterns of the hero Wonder Man and the body
of Jim Hammond.

00:03:24.310 --> 00:03:28.270
In other words, what on the surface seems
like just a Human Torch Easter egg in The

00:03:28.270 --> 00:03:30.810
First Avenger is also a Vision Easter egg.

00:03:30.810 --> 00:03:34.760
In fact, before the premiere of WandaVision,
some fans were theorizing this might be the

00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:36.349
key to how Vision would be resurrected.

00:03:37.349 --> 00:03:41.010
As soon as fans started catching glimpses
of images from WandaVision, it was obvious

00:03:41.010 --> 00:03:44.830
that the miniseries was taking part of its
inspiration from a few different comics in

00:03:44.830 --> 00:03:46.370
Vision's long history.

00:03:46.370 --> 00:03:51.599
The two most obvious inspirations are 1982
and 1985's The Vision and Scarlet Witch, two

00:03:51.599 --> 00:03:55.340
miniseries that see the odd couple move to
the suburbs of New Jersey to start a life

00:03:55.340 --> 00:03:56.340
together.

00:03:56.340 --> 00:04:01.120
Another obvious inspiration for WandaVision
is 2016's Vision series, which sees the eponymous

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:05.300
synthezoid building himself an entire family
of synthetics, all with names — like his

00:04:05.300 --> 00:04:10.030
own — beginning with "V." There's his wife,
Virginia, as well as his two kids, Viv and

00:04:10.030 --> 00:04:11.030
Vin.

00:04:11.030 --> 00:04:14.349
With the family he builds from scratch, Vision
tries to live an idyllic existence in a quiet

00:04:14.349 --> 00:04:16.230
suburb, but it isn't meant to be.

00:04:16.230 --> 00:04:20.269
They even eventually have their own synthezoid
dog named Sparky, built from the brain of

00:04:20.269 --> 00:04:24.389
a dog who accidentally electrocutes itself
while unearthing the corpse of a dead supervillain

00:04:24.389 --> 00:04:26.189
in Vision's backyard.

00:04:26.189 --> 00:04:30.009
Just as WandaVision's Sparky is killed in
"On a Very Special Episode...," the poor pooch

00:04:30.009 --> 00:04:33.219
of the comic book is also killed, though by
a different character.

00:04:33.219 --> 00:04:37.389
Paul Bettany even confirmed the connection
between the Disney+ show and the recent Vison-centric

00:04:37.389 --> 00:04:38.389
series.

00:04:38.389 --> 00:04:42.860
Speaking to Variety in January 2021, the actor
said the series was pitched to him as a mash-up

00:04:42.860 --> 00:04:47.620
between two comic book series – The Vision
and the 2005 miniseries House of M.

00:04:48.620 --> 00:04:51.949
In the mid-credits scene to episode 8, we
find Director Hayward standing triumphantly

00:04:51.949 --> 00:04:53.930
before a reassembled Vision.

00:04:53.930 --> 00:04:57.729
There's one major difference though — the
synthezoid is white from head to toe.

00:04:57.729 --> 00:05:01.509
While the visual is striking, it isn't the
first time the world has seen White Vision.

00:05:01.509 --> 00:05:06.169
During his time as Avengers chairman, Vision
secretly develops a plan to take over all

00:05:06.169 --> 00:05:10.440
of the world's computer systems, using them
to force peace and security on the world.

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:16.219
After being confronted by his teammates in
1985's Avengers #254, Vision relents and soon

00:05:16.219 --> 00:05:17.990
afterwards leaves the team.

00:05:17.990 --> 00:05:20.830
But some people aren't happy with the synthezoid
roaming free.

00:05:20.830 --> 00:05:25.580
In 1989's West Coast Avengers #44, Vision's
colleagues learn that he's been kidnapped

00:05:25.580 --> 00:05:29.659
by a team made up of rogue government agents
from different countries, that his body has

00:05:29.659 --> 00:05:33.310
been disassembled, and his memory has been
erased to ensure that he doesn't retain any

00:05:33.310 --> 00:05:37.629
of the sensitive information he had access
to in the 1985 Avengers story.

00:05:37.629 --> 00:05:41.289
Hank Pym is able to reassemble Vision in the
following issue, but he's nothing like the

00:05:41.289 --> 00:05:43.210
Avenger his teammates remember.

00:05:43.210 --> 00:05:47.460
Because of extensive damage to his circulatory
system, his skin is bone white.

00:05:47.460 --> 00:05:49.939
More importantly, he's lost all sense of emotion
and humanity.

00:05:51.090 --> 00:05:54.680
The White Vision of WandaVision isn't the
only callback to the time when the synthezoid

00:05:54.680 --> 00:05:57.379
becomes a lot less colorful in the comics.

00:05:57.379 --> 00:06:00.740
Towards the end of Avengers: Infinity War,
Thanos completes his quest by ripping the

00:06:00.740 --> 00:06:03.629
Mind Stone from Vision's head, killing the
Avenger.

00:06:03.629 --> 00:06:07.770
As soon as he does, all the color drains from
Vision's body, turning him gray.

00:06:07.770 --> 00:06:11.629
While, obviously, gray and white aren't the
same, in both cases, we get all other color

00:06:11.629 --> 00:06:13.629
leaving the synthezoid's form.

00:06:13.629 --> 00:06:17.249
Along with being a White Vision Easter egg,
this was one of many references in Infinity

00:06:17.249 --> 00:06:21.759
War to one of the comics that most heavily
influenced the film — the 1991 miniseries

00:06:21.759 --> 00:06:23.289
Infinity Gauntlet.

00:06:23.289 --> 00:06:27.619
When the small army of superheroes assault
Thanos in Infinity Gauntlet #4, Vision is

00:06:27.619 --> 00:06:31.319
among them, and it's the emotionless White
Vision who Thanos kills in the battle.

00:06:32.319 --> 00:06:36.309
Perhaps the most emotionally powerful Vision-related
Easter egg in the MCU comes in one of the

00:06:36.309 --> 00:06:38.879
flashbacks of WandaVision's eighth episode.

00:06:38.879 --> 00:06:40.479
Barging her way into S.W.O.R.D.

00:06:40.479 --> 00:06:43.340
headquarters, Wanda demands her right to claim
Vision's body.

00:06:43.340 --> 00:06:46.990
As she approaches his disassembled form and
tries to get some sense of his presence with

00:06:46.990 --> 00:06:48.240
her powers, Wanda says

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:58.199
"I can't feel you."

00:06:58.199 --> 00:07:01.899
This is a callback to the first of Vision's
two deaths at the end of Avengers: Infinity

00:07:01.899 --> 00:07:02.899
War.

00:07:02.899 --> 00:07:06.970
With time running out and Thanos on his way,
Vision convinces Wanda to use her powers to

00:07:06.970 --> 00:07:11.009
destroy the Mind Stone in order to keep it
out of Thanos' grasp, but there's no way to

00:07:11.009 --> 00:07:13.319
do this without also killing Vision.

00:07:13.319 --> 00:07:17.050
As Wanda begins the process, Vision tries
to comfort her.

00:07:17.050 --> 00:07:21.449
"You could never hurt me.

00:07:21.449 --> 00:07:25.270
I just feel you."

00:07:25.270 --> 00:07:29.220
Sadly, these turn out to be some of Vision's
last words to Wanda.

00:07:29.220 --> 00:07:33.339
While Thanos turns back time in order to restore
the Mind Stone, it's only to rip the Stone

00:07:33.339 --> 00:07:35.729
from the synthezoid's head and kill him a
second time.

00:07:36.729 --> 00:07:40.499
As soon as Vision came to life in Avengers:
Age of Ultron, comic book fans were wondering

00:07:40.499 --> 00:07:44.699
about whether or not they'd see a big screen
adaptation of Wanda and Vision's romantic

00:07:44.699 --> 00:07:45.699
relationship.

00:07:45.699 --> 00:07:48.289
It wouldn't be until the following year's
Captain America: Civil War that we got to

00:07:48.289 --> 00:07:53.099
see any kind of real romance developing between
the pair, but Age of Ultron gave us an enticing

00:07:53.099 --> 00:07:54.569
hint at more to come.

00:07:54.569 --> 00:07:58.710
Having left her post to destroy Ultron toward
the end of the film, Wanda misses her chance

00:07:58.710 --> 00:08:02.279
to board the Helicarrier and is still on the
airborne chunk of Sokovia when it hurtles

00:08:02.279 --> 00:08:03.279
toward Earth.

00:08:03.279 --> 00:08:06.009
Luckily, Vision flies through the debris to
rescue her.

00:08:06.009 --> 00:08:09.849
The visual of the synthezoid grabbing Wanda
and carrying her away had to give plenty of

00:08:09.849 --> 00:08:11.270
fans chills.

00:08:11.270 --> 00:08:15.110
It's particularly meaningful when you realize
there's a good chance Vision already has some

00:08:15.110 --> 00:08:17.099
semblance of attraction toward her.

00:08:17.099 --> 00:08:20.929
In order to have any idea where she is among
all the rubble and flying debris, he must've

00:08:20.929 --> 00:08:22.409
been keeping tabs on her.

00:08:22.409 --> 00:08:25.819
And why would he be doing that unless there's
something there that catches his eye?

00:08:26.819 --> 00:08:30.509
In a list of Vision Easter eggs, it would
be criminal to forget the faux TV ads featured

00:08:30.509 --> 00:08:34.690
in WandaVision, and the one that most heavily
references the synthezoid is the very first

00:08:34.690 --> 00:08:36.950
of the commercials we see in the miniseries.

00:08:36.950 --> 00:08:41.240
In episode 1, the story of WandaVision's black-and-white
world is interrupted by a commercial for the

00:08:41.240 --> 00:08:44.110
Toast Mate 2000, made by Stark Industries.

00:08:44.110 --> 00:08:47.900
Granted, Vision is definitely not the only
character being referenced here.

00:08:47.900 --> 00:08:51.710
When the ad's housewife starts the Toast Mate,
we hear a sound identical to the noise that

00:08:51.710 --> 00:08:54.040
Iron Man's repulsors make in the films.

00:08:54.040 --> 00:08:57.970
Plus, the matching pieces of toast are one
of the episode's many references to the twins

00:08:57.970 --> 00:09:00.370
who will soon arrive — Billy and Tommy.

00:09:00.370 --> 00:09:02.990
But Vision is the character who brings them
all together.

00:09:02.990 --> 00:09:04.800
Like the toaster, he's part machine.

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:09.290
He's Wanda's "mate," and, like the Toast Mate
2000, one of his creators was a Stark.

00:09:10.290 --> 00:09:13.720
One Easter egg in Avengers: Age of Ultron
doesn't reference the synthezoid directly,

00:09:13.720 --> 00:09:17.380
but instead, it's a little nod to one of his
real-world creators.

00:09:17.380 --> 00:09:21.301
When the Avengers clash with the combined
forces of Ultron and the Maximoff twins, Wanda

00:09:21.301 --> 00:09:25.360
uses her telepathic powers to force the heroes
into a nightmare world.

00:09:25.360 --> 00:09:29.160
In the case of Captain America, the super
soldier finds himself sent back to a World

00:09:29.160 --> 00:09:32.870
War II era dance hall, where we get flashes
of a celebration.

00:09:32.870 --> 00:09:37.500
It's tough to spot, but the band keeping everything
hopping is named after a Marvel Comics veteran.

00:09:37.500 --> 00:09:40.500
On the bass drum is the name "the Roy Thomas
Players."

00:09:40.500 --> 00:09:45.150
Among other things, Roy Thomas was the Marvel
Comics editor-in-chief after Stan Lee, and

00:09:45.150 --> 00:09:48.830
he's one of the two people, along with the
late artist John Buscema, responsible for

00:09:48.830 --> 00:09:51.310
creating both Vision and Ultron.

00:09:51.310 --> 00:09:53.530
Check out one of our newest videos right here!

00:09:53.530 --> 00:09:57.910
Plus, even more Looper videos about your favorite
comic book Easter eggs are coming soon.

